import "std/sha256"
import "std/text"

# The one benchmark whose PEAK is the point.
#
# Every other program in the corpus either holds its whole subject on purpose
# — the decoder and the encoder do — or is small enough to sit in the arena's
# first block. So the dimension "does the peak grow with the input" is
# weighted at zero by omission, and on 2026-08-31 that let a change that took
# an 8 KB digest from 79,691,776 arena bytes to 1,048,576 score exactly zero
# against the welfare floor. It also let the same change's 52x wall-clock
# regression through, because nothing in the suite runs long enough on an
# input this shape to notice.
#
# sha256 is a streaming algorithm: it walks the padded message sixty-four
# bytes at a time carrying eight state words, and everything a block builds is
# dead the moment the next block starts. So `digest_peak_bytes` is a constant
# in a compiler that reclaims and linear in the message in one that does not.
# It reads about ten thousand bytes per input byte today, which is the second
# number rather than the first.
#
# The message is read at runtime rather than embedded, the same reason
# bench/make_jsonbench gives: a compile-time fixture would let the optimizer
# fold the digest and flatter every row.
#
# One digest, not a loop. The peak of one run is the number this exists for,
# and a loop over a walk that never reclaims would measure the loop instead.
pub fn go raw
  print "digested: {text/slice (sha256/hex (text/bytes raw)) 1 16}"
